import {texEq,inlineMath} from './math.js';
// MoE-specific evidence recovered from the existing atlas. Model-wide details
// remain in their report notes. These cases are not universal training recipes.
const ref=(link,slug,label)=>`<a href="${link(slug)}">${label} →</a>`;
export function moeEvidence(link){return {
accounting:`<details><summary>Worked parameter counts: GLM-5 and GLM-5.3-Flash</summary>
<p>A gated FFN has gate, up and down matrices. With hidden width d and intermediate width h, these matrices store approximately 3dh parameters. Biases, routers and other layers require separate accounting.</p>
<div class="dense-table-wrap"><table><caption>Expert-matrix accounting from the existing report notes; approximate, not complete model counts</caption><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>GLM-5</th><th>GLM-5.3-Flash</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Hidden / expert intermediate width</th><td>6,144 / 2,048</td><td>4,096 / 2,048</td></tr><tr><th>Parameters per expert: 3dh</th><td>37.75M</td><td>25.17M</td></tr><tr><th>MoE / initial dense blocks</th><td>75 / 3</td><td>42 / 3</td></tr><tr><th>Routed / selected / shared</th><td>256 / 8 / 1</td><td>288 / 8 / 1</td></tr><tr><th>Stored expert matrices</th><td>75 × 257 × 37.75M ≈ 727B</td><td>42 × 289 × 25.17M ≈ 305.5B</td></tr><tr><th>Active expert matrices per token</th><td>75 × 9 × 37.75M ≈ 25.5B</td><td>42 × 9 × 25.17M ≈ 9.51B</td></tr><tr><th>Reported total / active model count</th><td>744B / about 40B</td><td>320B / 18B</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The difference between active expert matrices and the active model count is not unused work. Attention, dense FFNs and other components also execute. GLM-5's reported count includes MTP but excludes embeddings and the output head. GLM-5.3-Flash's dense FFNs add about 0.45B active parameters.</p><p>GLM-5's released configuration has 78 decoder blocks plus one parameterized MTP module. The report prose says 80 layers; the existing note records this unresolved counting difference rather than hiding it.</p>
<p>Both recipes normalize selected sigmoid affinities and apply a routed scaling factor of 2.5. Thus, a normalized mixture need not have a final weight sum of one. GLM-5 uses <code>noaux_tc</code> correction for selection. GLM-5.3-Flash computes router logits in FP32 and clamps expert activations: the gate has upper bound 10, and the up branch stays between −10 and 10.</p><p>${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#glm-block','GLM-5 accounting and router')} · ${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#glm-flash-experts','GLM-5.3-Flash accounting')}</p>
<p>Gemma 4 provides a smaller case: its 26B-A4B MoE stores 26B parameters and activates about 3.8B. The family also contains dense and edge variants. Do not treat every variant as the same architecture at a different width. ${ref(link,'models/compact-recipes#gemma','Gemma 4 variants')}</p>
<p>Active and total parameters are also separate scaling variables. A fixed memory budget may be divided between learned FFN experts and sparse retrieval tables. Calling a retrieved vector an “expert” is an analogy, not an identical execution mechanism. The scaling note discusses this allocation and labels its additive loss law as a hypothesis. ${ref(link,'training/scaling#gaps','Sparse capacity and memory allocation')}</p></details>`,
latent:`<h3 id="latent-experts">Latent experts: reduce the width of the routed path</h3>
<p>Fine segmentation increases the number of expert choices. A latent expert path reduces the width at which those experts operate. Kimi K3 projects the hidden state down to width 3,584, half its main hidden width.</p>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="K3 routed branch: hidden state, down projection, sparse latent experts, RMSNorm, up projection"><div>x · 7,168</div><span>→</span><div>Down projection</div><span>→</span><div>Experts · 3,584</div><span>→</span><div>RMSNorm</div><span>→</span><div>Up projection</div></div>
<p>K3 has 896 routed experts and selects 16 per token: ${inlineMath(String.raw`\frac{16}{896}=\frac{1}{56}`)}. Its two shared experts remain at full width. These counts describe different paths; do not assume that the shared experts use the latent width.</p>
<p>The narrower path reduces expert cost, but extra projections can increase activation scale. K3 applies RMSNorm before the up-projection and uses bounded SiTU-GLU activations. Quantile balancing controls load separately.</p>
${texEq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}\operatorname{SiTU\!-\!GLU}(g,u)&=\left[\beta_1\tanh\!\left(\frac{g}{\beta_1}\right)\odot\operatorname{sigmoid}(g)\right]\\&\quad\odot\left[\beta_2\tanh\!\left(\frac{u}{\beta_2}\right)\right]\end{aligned}`,`${inlineMath(String.raw`g=W_g z`)} and ${inlineMath(String.raw`u=W_u z`)} are the gate and up branches. Here, z is the latent input, and the two W matrices are learned projections. K3 uses ${inlineMath(String.raw`\beta_1=4`)} and ${inlineMath(String.raw`\beta_2=25`)}. The product symbol denotes elementwise multiplication. The soft caps limit extreme outputs while preserving similar behavior near zero.`)}
<p>MAI-Base-1 makes a different choice: select 8 of 512 experts, project down before dispatch, but compute routing from the original representation. It uses dropless routing and alternates dense FFNs with highly sparse MoE blocks. Its study reports comparable scaling and better wall-clock efficiency than more uniform MoE allocation on its stack. This is evidence for that layout, not a rule that every layer should alternate.</p>
<p>${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#kimi-depth','K3 Stable LatentMoE')} · ${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#mai-recipe','MAI architecture and scaling')}</p>`,
request:`<h3 id="request-routing">Request-level selection: when moving weights is the bottleneck</h3>
<p>Token-level routing assumes that selected expert weights are readily available. That assumption fails when most weights reside in flash storage. Loading a different expert set for every token can exceed the device's storage bandwidth.</p>
<p>Apple's AFM 3 Core Advanced stores a 20B sparse model in NAND flash and activates 1B–4B parameters. A lightweight dense block selects experts for the request, loads them into DRAM, and periodically reselects them during generation. A large shared path remains active.</p>
<p>Apple calls this Instruction-Following Pruning (IFP). It creates a request-conditioned subnetwork that runs for a period, rather than a fresh token-level top-k decision at every layer. Apple also describes a predetermined active-parameter budget chosen for task difficulty. The benefit is fewer weight transfers; the tradeoff is coarser selection.</p>
<p>The existing note does not disclose a complete expert layout, reselection interval or training recipe. Apple's cloud PT-MoE lineage is a separate design; do not infer its architecture from the on-device IFP description. ${ref(link,'models/compact-recipes#apple','Apple IFP and disclosure limits')}</p>`,
initialization:`<h3 id="routing-initialization">Initialization changes what the router sees</h3>
<p>A large random score advantage can concentrate the first updates on a few experts. Check raw logit scale, top-k margins and selected gate weights from the start. Equal assignment counts can hide experts whose selected weights are almost zero.</p>
<p>MAI identifies another source of early imbalance. Near-uniform attention initially resembles causal mean pooling, which makes token representations less distinct. Its recipe initializes attention output RMSNorm gains to zero. Cross-token interactions then increase during training, rather than dominating the initial router inputs.</p>
<p>Dense early layers can also delay routing until representations have been transformed. Neither approach guarantees stability. Compare them under the same quality and compute conditions. ${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#mai-recipe','MAI initialization')}</p>
<p>New structure tokens can disturb a pretrained router too. The Laguna-derived note initializes a new token from the average of its existing subtoken embeddings. It then warms up the input embeddings and LM head while freezing the rest of the model. This lets the new embeddings adapt before full post-training. ${ref(link,'post-training/sft#tokens','Special-token initialization')}</p>`,
rl:`<h3 id="rl-routing">Post-training: router drift and route replay are different problems</h3>
<p>Pretraining balance does not guarantee balance during RL. The policy sees a different data distribution, and router updates can change expert assignments quickly. MiMo-V2.6 provides a concrete failure case.</p>
<div class="dense-table-wrap"><table><caption>MiMo's trainable-router RL experiment: first 20 steps</caption><thead><tr><th>Load statistic</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Coefficient of variation</th><td>0.78</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><th>Peak expert load / mean</th><td>6×</td><td>16×</td></tr><tr><th>Cold experts</th><td>0.5%</td><td>22%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Restoring only the original router recovered balance without reducing benchmark quality. Freezing the router then kept load statistics stable while task performance improved. This isolates router drift in that experiment; it does not prove that router adaptation is always harmful.</p>
<p>MiMo Pro selects 8 of 384 experts; Flash selects 8 of 256. Both use an initial dense FFN and no shared experts in later MoE blocks. Hidden matrices use Muown, while the router, embeddings and LM head use AdamW. The report also records a rank receiving more than 30× the mean micro-batch token load.</p>
<h4>Recompute the same execution path</h4><p>Even a frozen router can select different indices when training and inference use different numerics. Then recomputed token probabilities may not match the execution path that generated the rollout.</p>
<p>MiMo records inference-time expert indices and reuses them during training through Rollout Routing Replay (R3). It also replays the exact top-p candidate set and applies MXFP4 quantize-dequantize after each update. Matching weights alone does not establish this consistency.</p>
<p>DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash retains expert-routing states when interrupted trajectories resume with a newer checkpoint. Its training concatenates the recorded routes from each segment. Such a trajectory spans policy versions; it is not a fresh rollout under one current policy.</p>
<p>K3 uses MXFP4 routed weights and MXFP8 expert activations, with higher precision for sensitive non-expert components. Quantization-aware training continues through SFT and RL to align rollout and training numerics.</p>
<p>${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#mimo-update','MiMo collapse and R3')} · ${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#deepseek-factory','V4.1 interrupted trajectories')} · ${ref(link,'training/frontier-recipes#kimi-depth','K3 deployment')}</p>
<details><summary>Related choices: adaptation targets and RL ratios</summary><p>Freezing the router does not freeze the experts. The LoRA note reports that adapting MLP/MoE weights outperformed attention-only LoRA in Thinking Machines' comparisons, even at matched trainable-parameter counts. Expert adaptation and router adaptation are separate choices. ${ref(link,'post-training/sft#choice','LoRA target selection')}</p><p>Sequence-level importance ratios can reduce sensitivity to isolated token-ratio outliers. The RL note reports Qwen's GSPO stability results for MoE policies. This changes the optimization objective; it does not replace route replay or repair an incorrect behavior probability. ${ref(link,'post-training/rl#variants','GSPO and behavior-policy consistency')}</p></details>`,
loop:`<h3 id="looped-experts">Reusing a layer does not require reusing its expert set</h3>
<p>A looped Transformer revisits the same physical layers. Its hidden state changes between visits, so the same router can select a different expert set. Sparse FFNs can therefore reduce the expressivity constraint of reusing one dense FFN.</p>
<p>The existing looped-MoE study compares four architectures: dense and sparse models, each with either 16 unique layers or 8 layers run twice. Its sparse blocks select 2 of 8 experts and use auxiliary balancing plus router z-loss.</p>
<p>Across most layers, only 4%–14% of tokens keep identical expert sets between the two passes. Disjoint sets occur for 25%–53%; most other tokens retain one expert and change one. Layer 7 has higher consistency. The authors suggest that it stabilizes representations before the loop-boundary vocabulary projection; this is a hypothesis.</p>
<details><summary>Experimental conditions and the evidence that limits this claim</summary>
<p>The study uses a 10B-token FineWeb-Edu sample, GPT-2 tokenization, AdamW, a Warmup-Stable-Decay schedule and μP. The backbone uses multi-head attention, RoPE, SwiGLU, pre-RMSNorm and residual connections. IsoFLOP budgets are 5 × 10¹⁶, 2 × 10¹⁷, 5 × 10¹⁷ and 10¹⁸.</p>
<p>The compute approximation is C ≈ 6ND, with active rather than stored parameters in N. Repeated passes still execute and must be counted. μP transfer from hidden width 128 to 1,024 gives less than 1% loss difference from the best learning rate across tested architectures. This does not establish clean transfer when depth changes.</p>
<p>Test-loss scaling ranks MoE, Looped-MoE, dense Base, then dense Looped. Base and Looped-MoE have fitted exponents 0.076 and 0.077. The similar slopes do not imply equal loss: Looped-MoE's curve is lower at matched compute.</p>
<div class="dense-table-wrap"><table><caption>Compute-optimal models at 10¹⁸ FLOPs; AI2 OLMES Core 9 average</caption><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Stored parameters</th><th>Core 9</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Looped</th><td>168M</td><td>37.4</td></tr><tr><th>Base</th><td>246M</td><td>38.7</td></tr><tr><th>Looped-MoE</th><td>216M</td><td>39.6</td></tr><tr><th>MoE</th><td>366M</td><td>36.4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>MoE has the best test-loss scaling but the lowest Core 9 score in this comparison. The authors propose that changing experts across loops improves expert coverage. The routing measurements support changing paths, not a proven causal explanation for all benchmark differences.</p>
<p>Training-free early exit uses vocabulary-output entropy, not router entropy. Looped models exit at loop boundaries; non-looped models can exit at intermediate layers. A logit-lens analysis measures Jensen–Shannon divergence from the final output distribution and finds convergence jumps at loop boundaries.</p>
<div class="dense-table-wrap"><table><caption>Perplexity at full depth and at 10% theoretical FLOPs saved</caption><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Full depth</th><th>10% saved</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Base</th><td>34.8</td><td>55.4</td></tr><tr><th>MoE</th><td>34.8</td><td>75.7</td></tr><tr><th>Looped</th><td>36.2</td><td>50.2</td></tr><tr><th>Looped-MoE · 8 × 2</th><td>35.9</td><td>51.0</td></tr><tr><th>Looped-MoE · 4 × 4</th><td>35.4</td><td>44.3</td></tr><tr><th>Looped-MoE · 2 × 8</th><td>37.3</td><td>42.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>More loops provide more candidate exits. The 2 × 8 model has worse full-depth perplexity but a better tradeoff at this saving level. MoE alone does not produce the same benefit. Low output entropy indicates confidence, not guaranteed correctness.</p>
<p>The largest active count is about 305M; stored MoE counts reach 711M. Effective depth is fixed in the main scaling study, which varies width. The reported savings are theoretical FLOPs, not measured serving speedups. Batching, dispatch, KV caches and hardware utilization still determine latency.</p></details>
<p>${ref(link,'models/recurrence#sparse','Looped-MoE study and recurrence context')}</p>`
};}
